<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yahya ibn Yahya al-Laythi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/848">848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yahya ibn Yahya al-Laythi was a jurist of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> descent who studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ibn-anas">Malik ibn Anas</a> and transmitted the most widely used version of Malik's al-Muwatta. Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, he became the leading jurist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>, influential under al-Hakam I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-ii">Abd al-Rahman II</a>, and did more than anyone to establish the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> in al-Andalus.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yahya ibn Yahya was born in 769 in the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>, into the Banu Abi Isa family. His grandfather Abu Isa Kathir, from whom the family took its name, was a Masmuda Berber soldier and a mawla, or client, of the Banu Layth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kinana">Kinana</a>, which gave Yahya the name al-Laythi. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a> rewarded Kathir with the governorship of Algeciras, then of Sidonia and again of Algeciras, where he died and was buried.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He travelled to the East while young and became an ardent follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ibn-anas">Malik ibn Anas</a>. Al-Andalus was then dominated by the followers of al-Awza'i, since most of the Arab conquerors had come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, beside other schools of law, as the historian al-Dhahabi notes in writing of Yahya's teacher Shabtun, Zaid ibn Abdarrahman al-Lakhmi.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> he devoted himself to scholarship. He never accepted a legal office, but as a member of the shura, the advisory council that the emir and judges had to consult, he gained great influence over appointments, became close to the ruler and grew to be its most influential member, able to nominate judges who favoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a>. At one point he was accused of taking part in a rising and fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> to live among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masmuda">Masmuda</a> tribes near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> al-Hakam I pardoned him and allowed him to return.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By the end of his life the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> was the most important in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. Yahya died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and was buried there on 22 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajab">Rajab</a> 234 AH. His descendants became one of the leading families of al-Andalus in the 9th and 10th centuries, partly through the fame of their ancestor, though several of them kept the family's power and influence by their own merits.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yahya ibn Yahya al-Laythi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Maliki school of law, introduced by scholars such as <b>Yahya ibn Yahya al-Laythi</b>, became dominant, giving the jurists of Córdoba great social influence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.13,-5.45&amp;year=838" aria-label="Algeciras on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4413 860 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yahya ibn Yahya al-Laythi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4863 1130h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4863 1130h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.13,-5.45&amp;year=838">What was here in 838 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
